The '''1972 NFL draft''' was the 37th annual meeting of National Football League (NFL) franchises to select newly eligible football players. The draft was held on February 1 and 2 at the [[JW Marriott Essex House|Essex House]] in [[New York City]], New York.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.footballgeography.com/nfl-draft-sites/ |title=NFL Draft Locations |author=<!--Staff writer(s); no by-line.--> |date=October 2, 2014 |website=FootballGeography.com |access-date=October 23, 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150905090250/http://www.footballgeography.com/nfl-draft-sites/ |archive-date=September 5, 2015 |url-status=dead }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Salomone |first1=Dan |title=NFL Draft headed to Chicago in 2015 |url=http://www.giants.com/news-and-blogs/article-1/NFL-Draft-headed-to-Chicago-in-2015/3ac322b1-3b4c-4a16-aba7-6e08d31b1756 |access-date=June 3, 2015 |publisher=[[New York Giants]] |work=Giants.com |date=October 2, 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150930065327/http://www.giants.com/news-and-blogs/article-1/NFL-Draft-headed-to-Chicago-in-2015/3ac322b1-3b4c-4a16-aba7-6e08d31b1756 |archive-date=September 30, 2015 |url-status=dead }}</ref>

With the [[List of first overall National Football League Draft picks|first overall pick]] of the draft, the [[Buffalo Bills]] selected defensive end [[Walt Patulski]]. Patulski would become viewed as a [[draft bust]],<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.espn.com/nfl/draft08/news/story?id=3325687|website=ESPN.com|title=Phillips couldn’t outrun off-the-field troubles|date=April 2, 2008}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|last=Graham|first=Tim|title=After 40 Years in the NFL Darkness, Walt Patulski Explains How It All Went Wrong|url=https://buffalonews.com/sports/bills/after-40-years-in-the-nfl-darkness-walt-patulski-explains-how-it-all-went-wrong/article_a40310fb-994d-5c7d-b719-d15dc1529c2c.html|publisher=[[The Buffalo News]]|date=November 19, 2016}}</ref> after the Bills released him in 1976 — coincidentally replacing Patuski with second overall choice [[Sherman White (American football)|Sherman White]] of [[1971 California Golden Bears football team|California]]. NFL historians have frequently described 1972 as one of the league's worst draft classes ever,<ref>{{cite web|last=Holder|first=Larry|title=NFL Draft’s all-time best and worst classes: No. 1 is clear, but recent classes are climbing|url=https://www.nytimes.com/athletic/3257259/2022/04/23/nfl-draft-history-best-worst-classes/|website=The Athletic|date=April 23, 2022}}</ref><ref name="Best">{{cite web|last1=Stuart|first=Chase|title=Best and Worst Drafts since 1970 |url=https://www.footballperspective.com/best-and-worst-drafts-since-1970/|website=Football Perspective|date=May 17, 2014}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|last=Rosa|first=Elwood|title=Ranking the 10 Worst NFL Draft Classes of All-Time — The Biggest Busts|url=https://www.sportingfree.com/football/worst-nfl-draft-classes-of-all-time/|publisher=Sporting Free|date=September 28, 2023}}</ref> especially regarding the failure of the majority of 1st round draftees, which has become viewed worse than other notably weak drafts like [[2002 NFL draft|2002]] and [[2009 NFL draft|2009]].<ref>{{cite web|last=Kacsmar|first=Scott|title=What Was the Worst NFL Draft of the New Millennium?|url=https://bleacherreport.com/articles/1245498-what-was-the-worst-nfl-draft-of-the-new-millennium|publisher=[[Bleacher Report]]|date=June 3, 2012}}</ref><ref name="Best"/> Only seven of 26 first rounders made the Pro Bowl in their career, and the whole 1972 draft produced just two Hall of Fame members, one of whom, wide receiver [[Cliff Branch]], was inducted posthumously. This was the fewest of any draft class since the [[1959 NFL draft|1959 draft]], which had only [[Dick LeBeau]] enshrined in Canton.

